{"term": "tener un lío", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "love_affair", "concept": "love_affair", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "euphemism", "crude": 15, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 92, "recognition_pct_peak": 92, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Idiom meaning to have a secret romantic or sexual affair; 'lío' literally means 'mess' or 'tangle,' euphemistically implying an entangled romantic or sexual involvement.", "english_term": "to have an affair", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "'Lío' alone can also denote the affair partner or a casual fling; the phrase is deliberately vague enough to cover both emotional and purely sexual entanglements."}
{"term": "follón", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "intercourse_generic", "concept": "sexual_activity_general", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 40, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 72, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": "ES", "definition": "Colloquial peninsular-Spanish noun for a sexual romp or fuck, etymologically derived from 'follar'; primary meaning is 'mess' or 'commotion,' and the sexual sense is secondary but well-established in Spain.", "english_term": "a romp, a shag", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Primary sense (mess, ruckus) is pan-Hispanic; the sexual double meaning exploiting the 'follar' root is predominantly Peninsular. Often used for innuendo without committed sexual intent."}
{"term": "asunto de cama", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "sexual_relationship", "concept": "sexual_relationship", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "euphemism", "crude": 10, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 68, "recognition_pct_peak": 68, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Euphemistic phrase meaning 'bedroom matter' or 'bedroom affair,' used to refer obliquely to sexual activity or an ongoing sexual relationship between two people.", "english_term": "bedroom matter, bedroom affair", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Deliberately vague construction; can describe a one-time encounter or a sustained clandestine relationship depending on context. Tactful enough for use in mixed or formal company."}
{"term": "ayuntamiento carnal", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "intercourse_generic", "concept": "intercourse_generic", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 10, "technical": 65, "recognition_pct_current": 42, "recognition_pct_peak": 70, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Archaic legal and ecclesiastical term meaning 'carnal union' or 'carnal joining,' denoting sexual intercourse especially in the context of marriage law and annulment proceedings.", "english_term": "carnal union, carnal congress", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "'Ayuntamiento' ordinarily means 'town hall' or 'joining/assembly'; the legal-ecclesiastical phrase was standard in Inquisition records, marriage annulment cases, and theological treatises through the 19th century."}
{"term": "cópula carnal", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "intercourse_generic", "concept": "intercourse_generic", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 8, "technical": 85, "recognition_pct_current": 48, "recognition_pct_peak": 65, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Archaic clinical and legal phrase for 'carnal copulation,' used in ecclesiastical, juridical, and medical texts to denote sexual intercourse with full anatomical detachment.", "english_term": "carnal copulation", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Frequent in Inquisition trial records, canon law, and 19th-century medical and forensic treatises. The Latin root 'copula' keeps the register clinical; 'carnal' specifies the physical, non-spiritual dimension."}
{"term": "cohabitación", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "cohabitation", "concept": "cohabitation", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "clinical", "crude": 5, "technical": 70, "recognition_pct_current": 58, "recognition_pct_peak": 58, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Clinical and legal term for living together as a sexual or romantic couple outside of formal marriage.", "english_term": "cohabitation", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Feels bureaucratic or legalistic to most speakers; everyday Spanish prefers 'vivir juntos' or 'pareja de hecho.' Appears in census forms, legal codes, and notarial documents rather than casual speech."}
{"term": "lujuria", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "lust", "concept": "lust", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 20, "technical": 25, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 88, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "One of the seven deadly sins in Catholic moral theology; intense and unrestrained sexual desire or appetite.", "english_term": "lust", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Highly recognizable across all Spanish-speaking cultures due to biblical and catechetical transmission. Carries heavy Catholic moral freight; 'lujurioso/a' is the adjectival form. Used in literary and moral contexts more than in crude slang."}
{"term": "lascivia", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "lasciviousness", "concept": "lasciviousness", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 25, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 62, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Wanton sexual desire or lascivious conduct; the quality of being sexually immodest, licentious, or given to overt sensual indulgence.", "english_term": "lasciviousness", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Semantically adjacent to 'lujuria' but emphasizes external behavior and display over interior desire. Common in moral treatises, Inquisition proceedings, and Golden Age drama. Adjectival form: 'lascivo/a.'"}
{"term": "impudicia", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "licentiousness", "concept": "licentiousness", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 20, "technical": 25, "recognition_pct_current": 28, "recognition_pct_peak": 55, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Archaic noun denoting sexual immodesty, unchastity, or shameless behavior; the absence of sexual modesty or 'pudor,' especially in conduct.", "english_term": "immodesty, unchastity, licentiousness", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Directly from Latin 'impudicitia.' Common in patristic texts, moral theology, and Spanish treatises through the 18th century; largely extinct in modern usage. Distinct from 'lujuria' (desire) and 'lascivia' (display) in stressing the absence of shame."}
{"term": "deshonra", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "female_sexual_dishonor", "concept": "female_sexual_dishonor", "is_novel_concept": true, "register": "archaic", "crude": 20, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 80, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The social and moral dishonor accruing to a woman and her family through sexual transgression — rape, seduction, premarital sex, or adultery — within the Iberian honor-shame system.", "english_term": null, "english_translation_quality": "none", "notes": "Central concept of Golden Age drama (the 'dramas de honor' of Calderón, Lope de Vega). English 'dishonor' lacks the gendered, familial, and sexually specific dimension; 'ruined woman' is approximate but individual rather than systemic. The concept encodes a collective family shame triggered by one woman's sexual status, with no tidy English equivalent."}
{"term": "concubina", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "concubinage", "concept": "concubinage", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 15, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 72, "recognition_pct_peak": 72, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A woman who cohabits with and is sexually available to a man of higher status without being his legal wife; a concubine.", "english_term": "concubine", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Widely recognized from biblical transmission and historical literature. Modern usage is almost exclusively historical, literary, or journalistic (historical reportage). The institution was legally regulated in medieval Iberian law."}
{"term": "manceba", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "kept_woman", "concept": "sex_worker_archaic", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 28, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 32, "recognition_pct_peak": 68, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Archaic term for a mistress, kept woman, or lower-status concubine; a woman maintained by a man outside of marriage for sexual purposes.", "english_term": "kept woman, mistress, concubine", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "'Mancebo/manceba' originally meant simply 'young man/woman' (from Arabic 'mancab'); the female form acquired sexual connotations of availability. Appears in medieval legal codes and Golden Age literature including Cervantes. Largely extinct; modern readers may not parse the sexual meaning without context."}
{"term": "barragana", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "concubinage", "concept": "concubinage", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 20, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 18, "recognition_pct_peak": 58, "peak_year": null, "regional": "ES", "definition": "A woman who lived with a man in 'barraganía,' a semi-legal informal union recognized in medieval Castilian and Aragonese law that granted her certain protections without the full legal status of wife.", "english_term": "concubine", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Distinct from generic concubinage: 'barraganía' was a legally codified Iberian institution with specific rights (inheritance claims in some fueros). Etymologically from Arabic 'barragan.' Specialists-only term today; virtually no recognition outside medieval history contexts."}
{"term": "fornicar", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "fornication", "concept": "fornication", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 20, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 80, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "To fornicate; to engage in sexual intercourse outside of marriage, framed through a lens of religious and moral condemnation.", "english_term": "to fornicate", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Widely recognized from biblical and ecclesiastical transmission; occasionally deployed humorously in modern speech precisely because of its portentous register. The noun form is 'fornicación'; the agent noun 'fornicador/a' is also current."}
{"term": "pecado carnal", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "fornication", "concept": "fornication", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 15, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 74, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Carnal sin; in Catholic moral theology, any sexual act considered sinful — primarily fornication, adultery, sodomy, or onanism — framed as an offense against God and nature.", "english_term": "carnal sin, sexual sin", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Broader than 'fornication' alone: encompasses all sexual sins in Catholic taxonomy. Appeared ubiquitously in confessional manuals, penitentials, and homiletical literature throughout the Spanish-speaking world. Still recognized in cultures with strong Catholic formation."}
{"term": "acto carnal", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "intercourse_generic", "concept": "intercourse_generic", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 10, "technical": 55, "recognition_pct_current": 63, "recognition_pct_peak": 75, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Carnal act; an archaic legal, medical, and ecclesiastical euphemism for sexual intercourse, emphasizing the physical dimension over the emotional or moral.", "english_term": "carnal act, sexual act", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Standard phrasing in Inquisition records, colonial penal codes, and 19th-century forensic medicine. Still surfaces in some Latin American legal contexts. More distancing and clinical than emotionally charged."}
{"term": "desfloramiento", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "defloration", "concept": "defloration", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 25, "technical": 35, "recognition_pct_current": 48, "recognition_pct_peak": 68, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Defloration; the act of taking a woman's virginity through first penetrative intercourse, framed around rupture of the hymen.", "english_term": "defloration", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Botanical metaphor (stripping petals from a flower). Variant form 'desfloración' also circulates. Found in Golden Age literature, colonial legal codes, and 19th-century medical texts. Carries heavy honor-economy weight: defloration without marriage constituted 'deshonra.'"}
{"term": "himeneo", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "consummation", "concept": "consummation", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 5, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 28, "recognition_pct_peak": 55, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "From Hymen, Greek god of marriage; in Spanish literary usage, refers to the wedding, the marital union, and by extension the sexual consummation of marriage.", "english_term": "nuptials, hymeneal (archaic)", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Primarily a poetic synecdoche: the god's name stands for the rite and its consummation. Used by Lope de Vega, Góngora, and other Golden Age poets. In modern Spanish, 'himeneo' is essentially unrecognizable outside classical-education contexts; the anatomical cognate 'himen' (hymen) is far more current."}
{"term": "virgo", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "hymen", "concept": "hymen", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 20, "technical": 40, "recognition_pct_current": 42, "recognition_pct_peak": 62, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Archaic Spanish term for the hymen or, by extension, the physical state of virginity; primarily an anatomical reference to the membranous structure at the vaginal opening.", "english_term": "hymen, maidenhead", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From Latin 'virgo' (virgin maiden). Used in older medical texts and Golden Age literature where the hymen was treated as proof of chastity. Modern speakers recognize 'virgo' chiefly as a zodiac sign; the anatomical meaning requires context. Current medical term is 'himen.'"}
{"term": "bello sexo", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "venus_archetype", "concept": "venus_archetype", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 5, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 58, "recognition_pct_peak": 75, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Archaic collective euphemism for women as a sex, emphasizing their beauty and erotic appeal; the Spanish equivalent of the English 'the fair sex.'", "english_term": "the fair sex", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Patronizing 18th–19th century idiom treating all women as a unified class defined by attractiveness and desirability. Appeared widely in Enlightenment-era Spanish literature, journalism, and conduct manuals. Now considered sexist and archaic; occasionally deployed ironically in modern usage."}
{"term": "el tálamo", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "consummation", "concept": "consummation", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 5, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 32, "recognition_pct_peak": 58, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The marriage bed or bridal chamber; a poetic and literary metonym for the conjugal bed where a married couple consummmates their union.", "english_term": "marriage bed, bridal chamber", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From Greek 'thalamos' (inner chamber). Common in Golden Age poetry and drama as a metonym for marital sexual union. In modern Spanish, 'tálamo' primarily denotes the neurological thalamus (part of the brain); the nuptial meaning is effectively dead outside literary contexts."}
{"term": "concupiscencia", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "concupiscence", "concept": "concupiscence", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 15, "technical": 30, "recognition_pct_current": 52, "recognition_pct_peak": 70, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Concupiscence; in Catholic theology, the inherited disordered inclination of human nature toward sin, especially the irrational drive toward sensual and sexual pleasure.", "english_term": "concupiscence", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Key term in Augustinian and Thomistic moral theology; the Spanish form mirrors the Latin exactly. Recognition is concentrated among those with formal religious or theological education; general-population recognition has declined with secularization."}
{"term": "libídine", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "lust", "concept": "lust", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 25, "technical": 30, "recognition_pct_current": 22, "recognition_pct_peak": 42, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Intense sexual desire or libido; a Latinate literary term for lustful appetite, closely related to 'lujuria' but with a more visceral, sensory connotation.", "english_term": "lust, libido", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Directly from Latin 'libido.' Less common than 'lujuria' in Spanish; restricted to highly literary or scholarly registers. Some speakers conflate it with 'líbido' (the psychoanalytic term). Stress falls on the second syllable: li-BÍ-di-ne. Very low modern recognition outside classical literature contexts."}
{"term": "lujuriar", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "lust", "concept": "lust", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 25, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 18, "recognition_pct_peak": 42, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "To lust; to act on or be consumed by carnal desire; the verbal form of 'lujuria,' meaning to engage in or be driven by lustful behavior.", "english_term": "to lust, to be licentious", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Rarely encountered in modern Spanish; 'lujurioso/a' (adjective) and 'lujuria' (noun) survive in active use while the verb has retreated almost entirely to archaic literary texts. Conjugates as a regular -ar verb but practically unused in speech or contemporary writing."}
{"term": "carne flaca", "lang": "es", "script": "latin", "romanization": null, "category": "religious_moral_status", "subcategory": "carnal_desire", "concept": "carnal_desire", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 10, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 62, "recognition_pct_peak": 68, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Literally 'weak flesh'; a religious idiom for the human body's inherent vulnerability to sexual temptation, drawn from Catholic doctrine on the frailty of physical human nature.", "english_term": "the weakness of the flesh, the flesh is weak", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Derives from the biblical phrase 'el espíritu está dispuesto, pero la carne es débil' (Matthew 26:41, Vulgate: 'caro autem infirma'). Used rhetorically to excuse or contextualize sexual sin by attributing it to bodily frailty rather than willful vice. Still widely understood in Catholic-formation cultures; occasionally ironic in modern usage."}
